---
title: "Everyone Was Right: Lexical Representations in Language Models Are Both Abstract and Holistic"
author:
  - name: "Zachary Nicholas Houghton"
    affiliations:
      - name: "Vail Systems, Inc."
      - name: "University of Oregon"
    email: znh@uoregon.edu
abstract: |
  Lexical representations are central to linguistic theory, yet to what extent they're memorized, item-specific representations or abstract, generalizable representations remains a core debate. In the present study, we investigate how Large Language Models (LLMs) navigate this distinction using a quantitative framework that decomposes contextual word representations into three additive components: an item component, a class component, and an idiosyncratic word-class interaction. After validating our measurement on an artificial language, we evaluate six pretrained transformers (OPT-BabyLM and Pythia families) across two linguistic categories: part of speech and grammatical role. We find that the idiosyncratic interaction term is consistently non-zero and grows substantially in deeper layers. Crucially, while abstract lexical and class axes remain nearly orthogonal, the interaction term systematically overlaps with both, allowing a probe to recover the class even after removing shared category directions. Together, our results demonstrate that LLMs do not commit exclusively to pure abstraction or exemplar storage; rather, they construct representations that are simultaneously both abstract and holistic.
  
bibliography: refs.bib
acl-mode: review
number-sections: true
tbl-cap-location: bottom
format:
  acl-pdf:
    keep-tex: true
