# Introduction

Lexical representations are deceptively complex: a single word often carries vastly different meanings depending on the context, yet these meanings remain recognizably related. The categorization literature has long recognized this tension, noting that assigning a novel meaning to a specific category (or word) is rarely straightforward. Simple rule-based approaches, such as rigid feature lists, quickly break down: a three-legged cat is undeniably a cat, even if it violates one of the criteria used to define the category. Ultimately, defining a lexical category in precise terms may be nearly impossible. Yet, as U.S. Supreme Court Justice Potter Stewart famously observed in another context, we may not be able to define a category precisely, but we nevertheless "know it when \[we\] see it."[^1]

[^1]: In *Jacobellis v. Ohio* (1964), Justice Stewart famously addressed the difficulty of defining obscenity, remarking that while formulating a precise definition might be a herculean task, he could still recognize obscene material when he saw it.

This central paradox, that representations are simultaneously context-specific yet broadly generalizable, has fueled long-standing debates over the nature of lexical representations. The two leading frameworks offer distinct solutions: **abstraction theories** posit that humans construct generalized mental representations stripped of specific context, while **exemplar theories** argue that representations are built from distinct, context-rich memory traces.

In the present study, we investigate how Large Language Models (LLMs) navigate this paradox. Specifically, we examine the extent to which LLM lexical representations comprise abstract, generalizable features versus context-specific exemplars.

## Evidence for Abstractions

Abstraction theories offer an intuitive account of the context invariance of language. At every level of language, there is substantial variability across contexts, from phonetic differences between speakers, to syntactic constructions that can take on a variety of lexical items, to the same word taking on different meanings in different contexts. The existence of abstractions accounts for the ability of humans to generalize across these contexts and recognize the same underlying representation despite this variability. Evidence for abstraction comes from linguistic productivity [@berkoChildsLearningEnglish1958], semantic extension [@harmon2017puttingoldtools], priming, word order preferences, and other phenomena.

Even something as deceptively simple as word order preferences, such as binomial ordering preferences, is shaped by abstract preferences [@morgan2016abstractknowledgedirect; @morgan2024productiveknowledgeitemspecific; @morganModelingIdiosyncraticPreferences2015]. For example, humans have strong preferences for binomial orderings such as *bread and butter* over *butter and bread*, or *bishops and seamstresses* over *seamstresses and bishops*. Those preferences are to some extent driven by abstract preferences such as a preference for male-coded words before female-coded words. Crucially, speakers' preferences for binomials that they have never encountered before are driven by these abstract properties, so the preferences cannot be stored with any particular pair of words.

## Evidence for Exemplars

Exemplar theories, in contrast, argue that abstract representations are not necessary. Instead, linguistic knowledge may be stored as context-rich memory traces whose properties vary with experience. Evidence for this view comes from both the gradient perception of phonetic cues [@mcmurrayGradientSensitivityWithincategory2008] and the representation of larger linguistic units, including multi-word phrases [@morgan2024productiveknowledgeitemspecific; @bybee1999usagebasedphonology; @houghton2025roleabstractrepresentations; @morgan2016abstractknowledgedirect; @houghton2026holisticstorageverb+up; @houghton2023doespredictabilitydrive].

More specifically, there's evidence that humans are sensitive to within-category changes in voice onset time [@mcmurrayGradientSensitivityWithincategory2008], produce *don't* in a more reduced manner in high-frequency phrases than in lower-frequency phrases [@bybee1999usagebasedphonology], have stronger word order preferences for high-frequency binomials [@morgan2024productiveknowledgeitemspecific], and recognize *up* less well in high-frequency and high-predictability V+*up* phrases [@kapatsinski2010frequencyuseleads; @houghton2025multiwordrepresentationsminds]. These findings suggest that linguistic representations retain information specific to the contexts in which their components occur, rather than being fully abstracted across contexts.

Most of the evidence above concerns sequences of words, but the same question arises within a single word. Whether regularly inflected forms are retrieved whole or assembled from a stem and an affix has been debated on much the same grounds, with frequency again playing a central role [@stembergerAreInflectedForms2004; @stembergerFrequencyLexicalStorage1986]. But the same logic applies even when there is no overt morphological combination. *Walk*, for example, occurs as both a noun and a verb. If the representation of *walk* as a verb was fully determined by a representation of *walk* together with a representation of the verb category, then nothing in that representation would be specific to that pairing. If instead some part of the representation is unique to *walk* as a verb, then the representation contains information about a particular combination that cannot be reduced to its parts.

## Present study

The evidence for both abstraction and item-specificity therefore raises a broader question about the structure of linguistic representations: is a representation of a specific linguistic construction a combination of abstract features, or does that representation also contain item-specific knowledge that is irreducible to these component parts? This is difficult to settle behaviorally, since a learner with no abstract representations at all can look like one that abstracts [@houghton2026exemplars], but it is a property of a representation, and unlike in humans, model representations can be examined directly [@pluthInterpretabilityWhisperEncodings2026; @elhage2022superposition].

We hypothesize that linguistic representations can contain both abstract and item-specific information. We therefore decompose a word-in-class representation into three components: the item-component, the component that generalizes across different classes of the same word (the part of *walk* as a noun that is shared by *walk* as a verb), the class-component, the component that generalizes across all items of the same class (e.g., the part of *walk* as a noun that is shared by *run* as a noun, and *plant* as a noun) and what belongs to that word in that class alone (e.g., the part of the representation unique to *walk* as a noun). We first validate this decomposition in an artificial language with controlled representational structure, then apply it to six pretrained language models across part of speech and grammatical role.

Our main contributions are as follows:

-   We introduce a method for decomposing word-in-context representations into lexical, grammatical, and word-class components, and validate it in an artificial language.

-   Across six pretrained models, the word-class component is consistently present and grows with depth, with its size governed more by representational capacity than by language. The lexical and grammatical components occupy nearly separate axes, while the word-class component overlaps with both.

-   We show that the word-class component contains information about the class itself beyond the shared class representation.

# Experiment 1: Artificial Languages {#sec-exp1}

In Experiment 1 we test our measurement on an artificial language whose features we control. This allows us to validate our measurement while simultaneously examining how different features of the language affect a model's representations.

## Dataset {#sec-exp1-data}

In order to examine the separability of linguistic representations, we constructed an artificial language which comprises a probability distribution over a vocabulary of `r cm(600)` tokens. Every observation is a combination of a **form** (one of `r max(toy$n_form)`), a **class** (one of `r max(toy$n_class)`), and a **context** (drawn from a pool of 400). The motivation for this setup is to give each word a naturalistic environment: the word occurs in a context that favors a specific class-membership (e.g., *walk* as a verb occurs in a different context than *walk* as a noun). Each form is observed `r max(toy$n_obs)` times in each class, every time in a different context, mirroring the patterns of human language. Consider *walk* used as a verb in one of those contexts. Four things determine the distribution over the next token. *Walk* itself contributes the same amount regardless of the class it is used in, and the verb class also contributes the same amount for every item. The third contribution is what is specific to *walk*-as-a-verb over and above those two: every form and every class has its own random vector, and this term multiplies *walk*'s by the verb's, entry by entry. The context contributes the rest, which is noise as far as our question goes, though the model still has to encode it in order to accurately learn the language. The four are weighted, summed and passed through a softmax layer:

$$
\begin{aligned}
&P(\text{next token} \mid f, c, x) = \\
&\quad \operatorname{softmax}\Big( s\big[\, w_\alpha\,\text{item}(f)
  + w_\beta\,\text{class}(c) \\
&\qquad + w_\gamma\,\text{int}(f,c)
  + w_{\text{ctx}}\,\text{context}(x) \,\big] \Big)
\end{aligned}
$$ {#eq-toy}

Each term is isotropically scaled to unit variance before it is weighted,[^2] so changing the weights changes the mix of the three effects without changing how much signal there is in total.

[^2]: Specifically, each observation consists of a form in a specific class and context, paired with that term's raw contribution to the next-token distribution for that observation. We normalize this dataset by dividing each value in it by the total standard deviation across all observations. This is necessary because different matrices can contain the same information at different scales. For example, imagine a matrix with random numbers, and that same matrix with each number multiplied by two. The information is the same, but the weights will have a larger effect on the latter dataset.

The weights $w_\alpha$, $w_\beta$, and $w_\gamma$ in @eq-toy control how much of the true distribution each of the three effects contributes, and we cross them factorially over $\{0, 0.5, 1, 2\}$: zero removes an effect from the language altogether, and the other three values make an effect half as strong as another, equal to it, or twice it. Similarly, $w_{\text{ctx}}$ governs how much the context contributes, so we cross those languages with three context weights: $\{0.5, 2.0, 6.0\}$, ranging from context being a minor part of the true distribution to the dominant one. These terms are subsequently scaled to unit length, such that their squared lengths sum to 1.[^3] The ranks of the item and class are held fixed at `r unique(toy$r_item)` and `r unique(toy$r_class)` respectively but each language is crossed with four different ranks of the interaction: $\{4, 16, 64, 177\}$. Additionally, we train a model on each of the resulting languages at hidden widths of `r paste(sort(unique(toy$d)), collapse = ", ")`, with either a linear or a ReLU activation. The whole grid is then run with five random seeds giving `r format(nrow(toy), big.mark = ",")` runs in all, of which `r sprintf("%.1f%%", 100 * mean(toy$converged))` trained to convergence; Appendix \ref{sec-appendix-grid} lists every factor and its levels.

[^3]: This makes the resulting languages more comparable by enforcing the same total signal (i.e., the variance of the weighted sum in @eq-toy is held constant), despite individual differences in the amount that each individual term contributes.

## Method {#sec-exp1-method}

All code and analyses in this paper can be found here: <https://github.com/znhoughton/representation-separability>.

### The learner

The learner model is a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) with a single hidden layer of width $d$ and either a linear or a ReLU activation function. The model takes as its input three pieces of information: form, class, and context. Each has its own embedding, and the three are concatenated and passed through the model which then outputs a probability distribution over the vocabulary (@fig-training). The training objective is thus to minimize cross-entropy between the model's predicted distribution over the vocabulary and the ground-truth distribution.

![Model structure. Every form is observed 24 times at each class, each time in a different context; that variation across contexts is what the measurement later splits in two.](fig-training.pdf){#fig-training width="100%" fig-env="figure*"}

### Decomposing a representation {#sec-measure}

We measure separability by decomposing a word-in-context representation into a lexical component, a class component, and an idiosyncratic component. The representation is the MLP's hidden layer, which for a given form and class varies across the contexts it is observed in. Let $M[i,c] \in \mathbb{R}^d$ be the mean of those hidden states for item $i$ used at class $c$. We decompose those item-by-class means by centering, so that:

$$
M[i,c] \;=\; \mu + \alpha(i) + \beta(c) + \gamma(i,c),
$$ {#eq-decomp}

where $\mu$ is the grand mean; $\alpha(i)$, item $i$'s mean across classes minus $\mu$, is the representation unique to the item; $\beta(c)$, the mean across items at class $c$ minus $\mu$, is the representation unique to the class (e.g., noun vs verb); and $\gamma(i,c)$ is the item-specific representation in a specific class, what item $i$ at class $c$ holds that neither marginal accounts for (@fig-measurement). Because every item is observed in every class with equal weight, averaging over class isolates the item effect, and averaging over items isolates the class effect, without distortion. The four terms add back to the typical representation for a given item in a given class ($M[i,c]$) exactly, and no two of them vary together across those representations, so the squared lengths of each term (excluding $\mu$) sum to the total and we report each as a share.

Each of these averages is taken over a sample of observations that differ only in context, so each carries some of the context term of @eq-toy as noise. We remove it by dividing the observations into two even-sized splits, running the decomposition on each split separately, and estimating each component's size as the dot product of its two split estimates, which is large when the splits agree about the component and near zero when they do not. As a result, noise that differs between the splits is independent and cancels.

For each representation we report two measurements. The first measure is the fraction of the representation accounted for by lexical, class, and idiosyncratic information: each component's squared length, summed over its vectors and divided by the total, so that the three fractions sum to one. The second measure quantifies the extent to which those components occupy the same directions. Specifically, we project the item vectors onto the class direction and record the fraction of their squared length that survives, and we project the interaction onto the smallest subspace containing both the item and the class vectors (i.e., the span of the two subspaces).[^4] A representation is separable if a word's representation is the sum of an item part and a class part, each on its own axis. Separability can fail in two ways: the parts may be non-orthogonal, so that projecting out the class also removes item information, or there may be a third component that is neither, whatever its orientation. The question is accordingly not whether class information is present, but how it is represented: on a single direction shared across words, or on a separate direction for each of them.

[^4]: Since there are only two classes, $\beta$ spans at most one direction and its projection into the item subspace, which has many more dimensions, has high overlap by chance; we therefore do not report it.

To quantify whether a size is meaningful, we compare each against zero, which is the null because the dot product of two independent split estimates has an expected value of zero if there is no structure in the underlying representations. We redraw the split two hundred times and call a size meaningful when its confidence interval excludes zero; for components whose value is exactly zero this flags an effect about 5% of the time (Appendix \ref{sec-appendix-validate}), the false-positive rate expected of a 95% confidence interval.

To quantify whether an overlap is meaningful, we project the effect, held exactly as measured, onto two hundred random subspaces of the same rank, which gives the distribution of overlaps an unrelated subspace would yield. An overlap below that baseline indicates meaningful orthogonality and one above it meaningful non-orthogonality. We report each overlap as a multiple of the baseline's median, with how many measurements fall outside its 95% interval.

![The four item-by-class means formed by two items in two classes. Under an item part plus a class part, three corners fix the fourth and the parallelogram closes. The arrow is $\gamma(i,c)$, the displacement between the fourth mean and the point that additive account places it at, with length $\lVert \gamma(i,c) \rVert$.](fig-measurement.pdf){#fig-measurement width="78%"}

It's important to note that the weights in @eq-toy describe the distribution the model is trained to match, while the measure decomposes the representation that the model learns, so the two sets of numbers are not on a common scale: a learner handed a language that is 85% interaction can represent it with the interaction taking a far smaller share. Appendix \ref{sec-appendix-validate} reports a separate check in which components were set directly in a representation and recovered almost exactly, which is what licenses reading the results below as facts about the learner rather than about the measure. We include runs only if at least one of the three components has a re-split interval excluding zero; where none does, the three shares divide noise and whichever lands slightly positive takes all of it.

### Analysis {#sec-exp1-analysis}

Each component's share of the representation is bounded between zero and one and takes both bounds exactly, so we model it with an ordered beta regression [@kubinec2023ordbeta] in *brms* [@burkner2017brms] with default priors, fitting one model per measured component and activation. Writing $y_k$ for the share component $k$ takes and $x_k$ for that component's size in the language,

$$
y_k \;\sim\; x_k \times \log_2 d \times \log_2 r \;+\; \sum_{j \neq k} x_j \;+\; (1 \mid \text{language}),
$$ {#eq-ordbeta}

where $d$ is the hidden width and $r$ the rank of the structure in the language. All predictors are centered, and a language is a unique combination of the generating weights and the interaction's rank, so the five random seeds of one combination share an intercept. Width and rank enter separately rather than as their ratio, which is not estimable alongside them; the claim that only the ratio matters is then the hypothesis that the two coefficients are equal and opposite. The two overlaps are modeled the same way with a Gaussian family, the outcome being an overlap divided by its own chance level and logged, and are conditional on a component being large enough to have a direction. Experiment 2 is not modeled this way, its six models being fixed architectures with nothing crossed.

